 — which makes sense if they have a strong hand and want to trap, or if they are unsure.\n\nBut if they had a monster, sometimes players bet for value. However, checking back with a full house is also common to trap.\n\nAlternatively, maybe they had a draw (like flush draw with clubs) and missed, and gave up.\n\nBut turn raise suggests strength.\n\nNow, your two pair is good, but likely behind a full house.\n\nWhat about straight possibilities?\n- Only possible straight is 5-6-7-8-9.\n- You have 7 and 8 — so if opponent had 6-7 or 6-10, they’d have the straight.\n\nBut did they play it this way?\n\nUnlikely they would slow-play pre-flop and flop, then raise turn with a straight draw.\n\nMore likely, they have a set or two pair themselves.\n\nBut here’s the key: **if you check, you go to showdown**.\n\nIf you bet, you risk them folding worse hands — but how many worse hands are there?\n\nWorse than 8s and 5s:\n- One pair hands (like A-x, 9-x, etc.) — but would they call a bet?\n- Possibly, but opponent raised on turn — unlikely with just top pair.\n\nSo likely, either:\n- They have a better two pair (e.g., A-5, 9-5, 5-8, etc.) → full house\n- Or they have trips (5s or 8s)\n- Or they have A-A or 9-9 → two pair or set\n- Or bluff/missed draw\n\nBut given the turn raise, it's unlikely they’re weak.\n\nTherefore, your two pair is probably **second best**.\n\nBut — if they are bluffing or have a weaker hand, you might get value from worse two pair (like A-9, making A-9-9-8-8? No, no 9s on board — only one 9. So no two pair with 9s unless they have pocket 9s.\n\nBoard: 8, 9, A, 5, 5.\n\nSo two pair possibilities:\n- 8s and 5s (you have that)\n- 9s and 5s (if they have 9-x)\n- As and 5s (if they have A-x)\n- 8s and 9s (if they have 8-9)\n\nBut anyone with 5-x has **full house**: 5s full of 8s or whatever.\n\nAh! Critical point: **any player holding a 5 in their hand has a full house** (trips 5s + pair 8s or 9s or A).\n\nSimilarly, holding an 8? Then they have at least two pair (8s and 5s), same as you — but with better kicker possibly.\n\nBut if they have 5-x, they have full house.\n\nGiven the turn card was 5♠ — which likely improved someone holding a 5 or completed sets — and opponent raised, it's very likely they have **at least two pair, likely a full house**.\n\nMoreover, they checked on the river — possibly to **induce a bluff**.\n\nIf you bet now, you may be **bluffing into strength**.\n\nAlternatively, they could be giving up with second-best hand — but turn raise makes that less likely.\n\nMost reasonable conclusion: **you are behind**.\n\nTherefore, best action is to **check back** and minimize loss.\n\nChecking allows showdown without putting more chips in.\n\nRaising (say 100 or more) will likely only get called by better hands (they’ll call with full house), or fold worse (but few worse hands exist).\n\nEven if they have A-A, they have two pair (Aces and 5s) — which beats your 8s and 5s (kicker doesn’t matter — two pair ties are split by higher pair first: 5s and 8s vs 5s and Aces → Aces win).\n\nSo even A-anything beats you.\n\nOnly hands you beat: those with nothing better than one pair, but who raised on turn? Unlikely.\n\nThus, **your hand is likely losing**.\n\nBut you can’t fold yet — checking is free.\n\nSo best move: **check** and see showdown.\n\nReason: Your two pair is strong, but the board and betting history suggest opponent has a full house or better two pair. Checking avoids inflating the pot when behind. Folding loses equity unnecessarily if you're wrong. Raising risks more chips with little chance of getting better hands to fold or worse hands to call.\n\nAnswer: 0\n\nFinal decision:\n\nEvaluation: You have two pair (8s and 5s) with 7 kicker. The board is paired twice (5s and 8s present), and opponent raised on the turn — a card that likely completed full houses. They checked on the river, suggesting either a trap or weakness, but turn aggression makes strength more likely. Few worse hands would call a bet, and better hands will call. Thus, no value in betting. Checking is safe and low-risk.\n\nReason: To avoid losing more chips when likely behind, while still realizing equity by seeing showdown.\n\nAnswer: 0"}
