nd very limited outs. The only possible draws are:\n\n- A gutshot straight draw (needing a 2 or 6 to complete a 2-3-4-5-6 or 3-4-5-6-7, but neither is currently viable with the board).\n- A backdoor flush draw (you have two hearts, but only three hearts on board would help, and even then, it would require two more hearts — which is very unlikely).\n  \nCurrently, you don’t have a made hand or a strong draw. The best possible outcome would be hitting a runner-runner straight or flush, which has less than 5% chance.\n\nYour opponent went all-in on the turn (Round 2), indicating extreme strength. Given that they are all-in, they likely have a strong hand like two pair, a straight, a set, or a draw stronger than yours (e.g., K-T for a straight, or club flush draw with A♣/K♣).\n\nCalling (-2) means risking 19,900 chips with an extremely weak hand and minimal equity against your opponent’s likely range. Folding (-1) preserves your remaining stack, though small, but since this is the last of 20 hands, chip preservation doesn't matter unless you can win the final hand decisively.\n\nHowever, winning the game depends on having more chips than your opponent at the end of hand 20. You started with 20,000 and now have 1,900 (after losing 18,100 so far). To win the game, you must surpass your opponent's total. Since your opponent went all-in, they likely have fewer chips left, but we don’t know their exact stack.\n\nStill, calling with such a weak hand is highly disadvantageous. The expected value (EV) of calling is negative because you will lose the vast majority of the time.\n\nReason:  \nFolding minimizes further loss in a hopeless situation. While going all-in technically “maximizes” potential gain if you miraculously hit runner-runner cards, the probability is too low (less than 5%) to justify the risk. Given your poor hand and lack of realistic outs, folding is the rational choice to avoid throwing away your last chips on a near-certain losing hand.\n\nAnswer: -1"}
